409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 92 0 R
/T (Association vs predictive importance. An OLS coefficient describes the association between a variable and quality while the other variables are held constant, in the sample. Permutation importance measures how much the model's accuracy on unseen data depends on the variable. Density and residual sugar have large OLS coefficients \(-0.34 and +0.33\) with opposite signs because they are highly collinear, but they add little to prediction \(0.030 and 0.062\). Alcohol is the dependable finding: large in both views.)
/E (Association vs predictive importance. An OLS coefficient describes the association between a variable and quality while the other variables are held constant, in the sample. Permutation importance measures how much the model's accuracy on unseen data depends on the variable. Density and residual sugar have large OLS coefficients \(-0.34 and +0.33\) with opposite signs because they are highly collinear, but they add little to prediction \(0.030 and 0.062\). Alcohol is the dependable finding: large in both views.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 92 0 R
/T (Most useful variables. Alcohol is clearly the most important variable in both models \(about three times the importance of the next variable in regression\). Volatile acidity and residual sugar follow in regression; density and chlorides follow in classification. Differences among the weaker variables are small and should not be over-interpreted.)
